my friends grace mercy and peace from  God our Creator and from Jesus Christ  our Lord amen earlier this weekend Jeff  and I had a celebratory dinner to mark  the fact that he has put up with me for  six years of marriage we went to our  favorite Italian restaurant although  there were several empty tables in the  restaurant the hostess seated us at a  small table right next to a family who  was already enjoying their dinner and I  was pondering the menu and I could hear  them talking and soon realized that the  young women at the table was definitely  a Yale student and I tried hard not to  go into work mode so I zeroed in to the  menu again but nonetheless we were so  physically close to them I was  uncomfortable but at least the table on  the other side just 12 inches from my  right elbow was empty for a few minutes  then a father and daughter were seated  there and I could smell his Cologne and  I could hear her grumbling about school  and I heard the father say your mother  and inferred that the girl's parents  were divorced honestly I wasn't  eavesdropping we were just that close  together I love small quaint restaurants  with a few tables the food is usually  amazing and I realize it is my own  discomfort with eating so close to  complete strangers that is the problem  here and I realize this is a pretty  ridiculous thing how privileged I am to  be in a restaurant to have an  anniversary to celebrate and the fact  that I thought of wanting more space in  this context is just wrong but Ian just  assured us of our complete forgiveness  in Jesus Christ so I will let that go  and we'll just use this as a sermon  illustration you get my point though we  don't eat with strangers if you eat most  of your meals in a dining hall chances  are you'd rather sit by yourself or  certainly find a friend to sit with  rather than look out for that person who  is sitting alone and might seem a little  awkward  to go join for conversation it's fair  actually because it can be fairly  awkward to just walk up and sort of  barge in on someone who might be wanting  some quiet time but it still makes us  uncomfortable to think about it even  eating together with people we do know  family members or friends is on in a  decline mealtimes are more likely to  involve cars or iPhones or TVs then they  are conversation with other people  instead we check in with our loved ones  in little spurts little emails brief  skype conversations little facebook  messages but much less often over meals  than in past generations eating together  is a lost treasure and I use that  language that we here in 2nd Timothy  guard the good treasure and I think  about what we have lost we are losing a  lot when we don't sit with strangers to  share common food and drink if eating  together is in fact a lost art why do we  bother with world communion sunday is  this something that the church is  hanging on too much longer than the rest  of society just for the sake of  tradition well we have 10 kinds of  communion bread today just because it's  tasty no way we celebrate world  communion sunday here to remember why we  do communion at all because these are  the gifts of God and we are hungry world  communion sunday reminds us that we  share these gifts with others from all  around the world and we shared that  hunger for God as well globalization is  not news at Yale over two years ago Tony  Blair launched his faith in  globalization initiative we hear the  word globalization all over the place  here Yale is focused on being a leader  throughout the whole world students here  will have the chance to study abroad  look around we are an international  community even here today  a so I think we are already globally  minded this is not news but how often do  you actually think about Christian  communion with others around the world a  global day a globalization that started  long before we had a word for it as you  walk up the aisles to receive Communion  here do you ever think about a similar  meal happening in a church in Korea or  South Africa or Switzerland the way we  tend to come forward one by one open  hands here might give us the false  impression that it is just about an  individual and a tiny chunk of bread  dipped in wine when in reality it is  about God loving the whole world it is  about Christ's body made present to us  in bread and wine but also that Christ's  body includes people from all around the  world this is a meal in which we get to  eat with complete strangers we come  empty-handed to this meal because no  amount of money can buy these gifts even  if we have eaten breakfast we come to  this table with a spiritual hunger that  the grace of God only can satisfy and we  pray every week give us this day our  daily bread so the small amounts of  bread and wine we receive at communion  remind us that God does provide  sustenance enough for today yet  sometimes we doubt that it will be  enough that this little bit of bread  that these few drops of wine will  actually sustain us in Luke's Gospel the  disciples cry out to Jesus increase our  faith they had just heard a stern  warning from Jesus about the punishment  of those who stumble they had just heard  from Jesus a command to be on guard  always ready and the disciples respond  please increase our faith otherwise we  will not be able to do this  so how does Jesus reply  if you had faith the size of a mustard  seed the smallest speck you could say to  this mulberry tree be uprooted and be  planted in the sea and it would obey you  if it's not easy to tell if Jesus words  are meant to challenge or comfort the  disciples we don't exactly know the  inflection he was using was he scolding  if only you had faith the size of a  mustard seed you could actually move  this tree if only or was he comforting  them calm down a little bit dear  disciples you don't need to have faith  the size of an apple or watermelon or  Texas you need to have faith the size of  a mustard seed that's all do you know  how small mustard seeds are in the palm  of my hand I probably have 100 of them  they are tiny if you look at your  bulletin and find the dot of an eye that  would be about the size of a mustard  seed yet they're pretty hard to hold on  to someone came up and bumped into me up  here they would go flying or if I get  really animated in a minute and start  throwing my arms around they are going  everywhere it seems like having just  this tiny speck of faith should be easy  but you and I know that life happens the  trouble bumps into us that distractions  overwhelm us so Jesus increase our faith  make it easier to hold on to the  disciples requests to Jesus is an  important reminder that faith is a gift  we cannot force it upon ourselves or  others it is gift it is a small mustard  seed in a hand and then a cry out to God  when we look down and realize our hands  are empty and then looking down again to  realize God has provided  bit of daily bread so I think Jesus was  trying to both challenge and comfort his  disciples then and now on the one hand I  think Jesus does supply us with mustard  seed doses of faith and some days these  are easier to hold on to than others but  i think it is God who is in the business  of uprooting trees and moving mountains  we need to stay in the realm of metaphor  here because if you think about trees  crashing on the roofs of homes or  mountains crumbling in an earthquake we  label these acts of God yet it's pretty  dangerous theology to say that God was  punishing anyone who is damaged or died  in those calamities so we stay in the  realm of metaphor when we think about  moving mountains in the language of  Matthew or uprooting trees in the  language of Luke God is in the business  of large seemingly impossible  transformations on this earth and maybe  we have a small small hand in that if we  have had some chance to make some  difference in the world some positive  change in the world may be working  toward the cure of a disease may be  working for policy change that will look  out for our brothers and sisters in need  if we have had the chance to do anything  good it is by the grace of God it is God  working through us that lets these  things happen take a moment to think  about your earliest memory of Holy  Communion was it a ceremonial first  communion as a child did it happen later  in life what did you consume was it a  little dry wafer was it a hunk of bread  do you remember how the drink tasted or  were you watching from afar are these  memories that make you feel welcome and  included or maybe excluded what were  your earliest memories of communion  world communion sunday is where these  memories that have shaped us meet  today's reality of Christianity we bring  those familiar stories of home with us  and remember that other's homes look  very different we remember that the  church is growing fast in some places in  being persecuted in other places on  world communion sunday we remember that  on altar tables around the globe  Christians are using a common starch and  a festive drink whatever those elements  look like and those cultures to remember  that on the night in which he was  betrayed Jesus gathered for a meal with  his friends this is a meal in which we  sit very close to strangers an  invitation and a choice we make to eat a  meal that no amount of money can buy and  to hold in our hands symbols of God's  grace for the whole world thanks be to  God in every language in every way we  can possibly say it thanks be to God  amen
 

